# API Clean Code & Performance

When writing routes, services, or repositories, prioritize readability, simplicity, and performance. Prefer the simplest correct solution; optimize for fewer DB roundtrips and sound algorithmic complexity.

## Layer responsibilities

- **Routes**: HTTP concerns only — parse input, call service, map response. No business logic or direct DB access.
- **Services**: Orchestrate use cases, enforce business rules, coordinate repositories. Keep methods focused and named after domain actions.
- **Repositories**: All SQL/Drizzle queries live here. Return only the columns needed; push filtering, sorting, and aggregation to the database.

## Database performance

- **Minimize roundtrips**: Batch related reads/writes. One query with a join or `IN (...)` beats N sequential lookups.
- **Avoid N+1**: Never fetch a list then loop with per-item queries. Use joins, subqueries, or batched `whereIn` instead.
- **Select only what you need**: Avoid `select *` when a subset of columns suffices.
- **Filter in SQL**: Apply `where`, `orderBy`, `limit`, and counts in the query — not in application memory after over-fetching.
- **Use transactions** when multiple writes must succeed or fail together.
- **Index-aware queries**: Structure `where`/`join` conditions to match existing indexes; avoid functions on indexed columns that prevent index use.

## Algorithm & memory

- Prefer O(n) single-pass transforms over nested loops when processing collections.
- Build lookup maps (`Map`, `Record`) once when joining in-memory data from two query results — O(n + m), not O(n × m).
- Do not load unbounded result sets; paginate or stream when lists can grow large.

## Clean code

- Name functions after what they do (`findManyByLocation`, `assertLocationBelongsToOrganization`).
- Extract non-obvious query logic into the repository; keep services readable at a glance.
- Reuse existing repository methods instead of duplicating queries.
- Handle errors at the service layer with domain errors; let repositories stay thin and throw only on DB failures.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — N+1: one query per item
const items = await repo.findMany(db, locationId);
const enriched = await Promise.all(
  items.map(async (item) => ({
    ...item,
    location: await locationRepo.findById(db, item.locationId),
  })),
);

// ✅ GOOD — single query with join or batched lookup
const rows = await repo.findManyWithLocation(db, locationId);
// or: const locations = await locationRepo.findByIds(db, ids);
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — two roundtrips for a check + update
const existing = await repo.findById(db, id);
if (!existing) throw new NotFoundError();
await repo.update(db, id, input);

// ✅ GOOD — one conditional update, check affected rows
const updated = await repo.updateById(db, id, input);
if (!updated) throw new NotFoundError();
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — business logic and SQL mixed in a route handler
app.get("/equipment", async (c) => {
  const rows = await db.select().from(equipment).where(...);
  return c.json(rows.filter((r) => r.active));
});

// ✅ GOOD — route delegates to service; repository owns the query
const result = await equipmentService.list(db, locationId);
return c.json(result);
```
